<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timurid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Timurid, Timurids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Timurid Empire was the state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in the late fourteenth century, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. After Timur's death it fragmented among his descendants but became famous for its patronage of Persian art, architecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> and literature. It was eventually overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, and the Timurid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> went on to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timurid-empire.jpg" alt="Timurid Empire" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DigitalNomadWikiKzRu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timurid_State.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
